RISC-V lifecycle development checks
==================================

From this source tree, run with a native C compiler:

  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/manifest_hook_test.sh
  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/entry_test.sh
  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/boot_order_test.sh
  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/start_tracking_test.sh
  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/hsm_test.sh
  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/shutdown_test.sh

These compile verbatim production functions against controlled kernel/firmware
boundaries. Manifest checks use the in-tree libfdt. No RISC-V emulator, kernel
boot or actual cache-coherence test is performed by these commands. They run
from the source tree, not an installed kselftest bundle. CC and TMPDIR can be
overridden; MK_SOURCE can select another compatible source tree.

Checks cover preparation before /chosen emission, a tree larger than PAGE_SIZE,
property-before-child ordering, allocation/mapping errors, all-hart tracking
before priming, failed-release retention, sparse hart IDs, HSM error translation,
bounded STOP_PENDING polling, and local machine halt/poweroff/restart routing.
Boot ordering and early panic-notifier registration execute setup_arch up to
sbi_init against controlled boundaries. Panic callbacks and crash shutdown are
checked, but not the whole panic(), remote IPI path or SBI reset registration.
The entry test substitutes instruction bytes: inspect entry.o separately for
fence.i, relocations and a0/a1 preservation. Fake barriers check program order
only, never hardware memory ordering.

Entry lifetime and priming
-------------------------

The host prepares two adjacent control pages before generating the manifest:
mutable entry data followed by immutable code. The stub starts with fence.i,
finds the preceding data page using its own PC, loads image_entry, and jumps
without changing a0 or a1. No relocation target is embedded in the copied code.
The primary receives the boot DTB in a1. A secondary receives SBI boot data in
a1; its Image entry is secondary_start_sbi, not the Image's primary entry.

A fence.i in a newly copied stub alone is insufficient: its first instruction
fetch may still see an earlier occupant of that physical page. Each assigned
hart first runs mk_riscv_entry_fence_stop from immutable host text, fences and
HART_STOPs. The host waits for STOPPED before using the copied stub. Pool entry
also fences before HART_STOP. This depends on the host text remaining reserved
and unchanged; it does not assume stopped harts accept remote fence requests.

An opaque entry record would avoid a mutable entry immediate, but a1 then holds
that record rather than the boot ABI's DTB/secondary data. Such a design must
restore a1 and still solve the first-fetch problem if its code page is recycled.
The current adjacent-page design preserves firmware-supplied a0/a1 directly.
The secondary maps host-owned data with memremap, rather than assuming that a
future no-map control reservation remains in the spawn's linear map.

Not a runtime-ready stack
-------------------------

This architecture series must land together. It is not safe to advertise as a
complete lifecycle implementation on this baseline. Shared resource serialization,
uncertain ownership retention and shutdown state settlement remain prerequisites
on master. In particular, a STOPPED snapshot does not cancel a queued remote
start. A caller returning a hart to the pool must fence outstanding work first.
The ACK reproducer/proposal is maintained separately; no wire policy is changed.

#26 supplies the Image loader; #27 uses the manifest itself as the primary a1
boot tree and emits assigned hart/intc nodes and control/boot-page reservations.
Do not layer the obsolete July host-DTB clone on the modern persistent manifest.
#28 supplies SBI doorbells and lifecycle
notifications. No #28 message is sent from these local machine stop paths. Without
that follow-up the host is not automatically notified about a voluntary stop.
No #7 dependency is introduced. Existing ring resets are not a proof that work
on surviving peers has drained or that old replies cannot cross a re-spawn.

With panic_timeout=0 the spawn panic notifier stops immediately. A nonzero
timeout follows the generic panic restart policy, but the spawn restart override
still uses local HART_STOP, never host-wide reset. There is no parent notification
in these local paths. Crash shutdown is guarded as well because crash kexec
can run before notifiers. Host panic policy remains unchanged. HART_STOP failure
leaves the hart in a local wait loop: host confirmation must fail and retain its
resources. Fresh QEMU/OpenSBI, x86 concurrency, failure-injection and 50-cycle
same/different-Image tests are still required after the dependencies are combined.
